{
  "abstract": "No rational Diophantine sextuple had been shown to be non-extendable. Earlier results, including the height-certified sieves of paper 5, only bound the height of a possible seventh element. We prove that six explicit rational Diophantine sextuples have no seventh element at all. For a sextuple $\\{x_1,\\dots,x_6\\}$, a seventh element $e$ gives a rational point on the genus-two curve $C:\\ y^2=\\prod_i(x_ie+1)$. All six Weierstrass points of $C$ are rational. We run a full 2-descent on $J(C)$ over the 1090 sextuples (up to sign) in the corpora of papers 5, 9 and 10. Exactly six have Selmer rank bound $\\le1$. For these, $J(\\mathbb Q)\\cong\\mathbb Z\\oplus(\\mathbb Z/2)^4$, generated by $J[2]$ and the class of the point $e=0$. Coleman integration at one good prime $p\\in\\{23,41,43,47,53,79\\}$ gives an annihilating differential that does not vanish mod $p$ at any of the eight known rational points. So each known residue disc contains exactly one rational point. A Mordell–Weil sieve with smooth modulus $L\\le6000$ over primes below 1500 rules out every other residue disc. Saturation at all relevant primes is proved. Hence $C(\\mathbb Q)$ consists of the eight obvious points, and $\\prod(x_ie+1)$ is a nonzero square only for $e=0$. In particular, none of the six sextuples extends to a rational Diophantine septuple. The smallest is $\\{-184/65,-5643/2080,-715/288,-54/65,481/1440,40/117\\}$. The method needs $\\mathrm{rk}\\,J\\le1$, which fails for the other 99.4% of the census. All 6534 quintic genus-two quotients we tested have Selmer rank bound at least 2.",
